    \caption{Conceptual illustration of a Web Crawler}
    \label{fig:crawler_concept}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Objectives and Scope}
The primary objective is to develop a high-performance, dependency-free Web Crawler optimized for the LAMP/XAMPP stack. Unlike traditional PHP scripts that suffer from memory leaks and slow execution, this system introduces an advanced architecture featuring:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Scalable Persistence:} Utilizing SQLite (via \texttt{database.php}) to manage millions of URLs without memory overflow, replacing volatile RAM storage.
    \item \textbf{Parallel Execution:} Implementing \texttt{cURL Multi-Exec} (via \texttt{multi\_curl.php}) to fetch multiple pages simultaneously, achieving 5-10x speed improvements over sequential crawling.
    \item \textbf{Access Control:} A built-in authentication handler (\texttt{login\_handler.php}) capable of logging into protected websites using standard HTTP POST requests with CSRF token extraction.
    \item \textbf{Intelligent Filtering:} Heuristic algorithms (\texttt{smart\_filters.php}) to automatically discard low-quality content (e.g., icons, banners) without requiring heavy AI libraries.
\end{itemize}

The scope is strictly defined to use \textbf{Pure PHP (Native Extensions)} to ensure ease of deployment and zero external dependencies (no Redis, no Docker, no Node.js, no Composer required).


