\section{Implementation Details}

This chapter provides an in-depth examination of the system's codebase, focusing on the specific algorithms and logic patterns employed to achieve robust crawling and file management. The implementation logic is distributed across the PHP backend for processing and the JavaScript frontend for state management.

\subsection{Crawler Engine Core Logic}
The primary processing unit is the \texttt{WebCrawler} class located in \texttt{includes/crawler.php}. Its architectural design emphasizes modularity and state isolation.

\subsubsection{Initialization and Environment Setup}
Upon instantiation, the crawler performs critical setup procedures to ensure a stable execution environment. The constructor normalizes the \texttt{baseUrl} to remove fragments and ensure a consistent schema. A unique cookie file is generated using \texttt{sys\_get\_temp\_dir()} hashed with the target URL, allowing the crawler to maintain session consistency across multiple requests, which is essential for sites requiring cookie persistence.

Furthermore, the system initializes a \texttt{RobotsChecker} instance immediately. This component fetches and parses the \texttt{robots.txt} file, storing disallowed paths in memory to prevent repetitive network lookups during the crawl cycle.

\subsubsection{Request Handling with Cookie Persistence}
To maintain session state across requests, the system uses a persistent cookie jar stored in the system's temp directory. The \texttt{fetchUrl} method configures cURL to save and load cookies automatically, which is essential for crawling websites that require session tracking or authentication.

