\subsection{Advanced Data Extraction}
The file discovery mechanism goes beyond simple HTML parsing. It implements a multi-layered strategy to maximize recall.

\subsubsection{DOM Traversal with Lazy-Load Detection}
The \texttt{extractLinks} method utilizes PHP's \texttt{DOMDocument} library. A significant enhancement in our implementation is the detection of lazy-loaded resources. Modern web frameworks often defer image loading by storing the actual URL in data attributes. Consequently, our parser iterates through a prioritized list of attributes—\texttt{src}, \texttt{data-src}, \texttt{data-original}, and \texttt{data-url}—to ensure the highest quality resource is captured.

\subsubsection{Heuristic Regex Scanning}
Recognizing the limitations of DOM parsers with invalid HTML or JavaScript-injected content, a secondary Regex scanner is employed. This scanner constructs a dynamic pattern based on the user's selected file extensions. It scans the entire response body to identify string patterns resembling URLs that end with allowed extensions (e.g., \texttt{.pdf}, \texttt{.mp4}), effectively uncovering resources hidden within JSON blobs or script variables.

\subsection{Archive Generation and File Integrity}
The bulk download functionality handles the complexity of aggregating multiple remote files into a single ZIP archive. The script \texttt{download\_zip.php} addresses a common issue in web scraping: filename collisions.

When multiple files have the same name (e.g., \texttt{image.jpg}), a naive implementation would overwrite them. Our system implements a collision resolution algorithm that appends a numerical counter to the filename (e.g., \texttt{image\_1.jpg}) before adding it to the \texttt{ZipArchive} stream. This ensures data integrity for the end user.

